#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120680} :activity/id #uuid "4f1a5ecd-cb6d-546d-a554-9b94e41a336a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050656 :topic/id #uuid "5ce2b050-dd59-54b3-bb85-3a4f08ea0365" :topic/math-academy-id 1850} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120680 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121037 :tutorial/content "The **column space** of a matrix $A$ is denoted $\\text{Col}(A)$ and is the span of the columns of $A$ (i.e., the set of all possible linear combinations of columns of $A$). For example, consider the matrix\n\n![](images/6c/6c03ab3dfd7b75c9a89defe102f33946da200fb88d26139e54c889a90467068a.svg)\nwhere we denote its columns by $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_1$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2},$ respectively. The column space of this matrix is given by\n![](images/5f/5faad23bc77f821d42223beaca06364e38c7f0ba8a4869cb138e0e923e90b8f7.svg)\n\nNow, suppose we would like to determine whether the vector ![](images/45/45349edee356334c54ad85a6d60757a6a7833a392962086815a2436446e323dc.svg) lies in $\\textrm{Col}(A).$ In other words, we need to check if $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Span}\\{ {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2} \\}.$ So, we need to determine whether $\\mathbf{b}$ can be written as some linear combination of $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_1$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the equation above can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =b \\\\ {{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{x}} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{b}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation is equivalent to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}.$ To solve the equation, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A \\,|\\, \\mathbf{b}]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ -7{x}_{2}=-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x_2=1.$ Substituting into the first equation, we get $x_1=-1.$\n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is consistent, which implies that $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Col}(A).$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $\\mathbf{b}$ as a linear combination of $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_1$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/a3/a3965a53b3b3cfda2e11c4a68ba882631dc2763f32de67de30b4751aca76f2e5.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "f65da145-207a-50c6-b59e-8756d247cc71" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3201 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e2b22098-86e0-53ea-bbc9-fc31b60b4152" :step/math-academy-id 17489 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120681}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120681 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119810 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119621 :question/id #uuid "244793f6-41c3-5920-9264-bec32cda9c9c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4807"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c8e217c-252d-529f-a810-84787e2ee886" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048315}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120339} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247182} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408163}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Vector is in the Column Space of a Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "415a39b4-7da9-55ac-afde-f8932b3c0813" :step/math-academy-id 17491 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120749}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120682 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121038 :tutorial/content "The column space is defined for matrices of *any* dimensions. In general, if ${\\color{black}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{black}\\mathbf{a}_2}, \\ldots, {\\color{black}\\mathbf{a}_n}$ are the columns of a matrix $A,$ that is to say,\n![](images/d9/d9d3fd7384b4375895d234c22c9d81f30358439f7e04ee8db055de9d5d7f1174.svg)\nthen $\\textrm{Col}(A) = \\textrm{Span} \\{ {\\color{black}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{black}\\mathbf{a}_2}, \\ldots, {\\color{black}\\mathbf{a}_n} \\}.$\n\nThe column space satisfies the following properties:\n\n- If $A$ is an ${\\color{blue}m}\\times n$ matrix, then $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^{\\color{blue}m}.$ This is because each column has ${\\color{blue}m}$ entries.\n\n- $\\mathbf{b}\\in\\textrm{Col}(A)$ if and only if the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is consistent (has a solution).\n\n- If the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is inconsistent (has no solution), then $\\mathbf{b}\\not\\in\\textrm{Col}(A).$\n\nThese hold for any $m\\times n$ matrix, and the matrix does not necessarily have to be square." :tutorial/id #uuid "f33b7df2-b0c3-5b23-b26b-50509cefd3a3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3203 :tutorial/title "Properties of the Column Space of a Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "65ce55e4-127b-59e8-96ab-fabcdc24aef9" :step/math-academy-id 17493 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120683}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120683 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119811 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119622 :question/id #uuid "12f222d8-5563-544a-8154-fb1f9406b231" :question/math-academy-id "e-4808"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87dc8398-fb5c-564b-a796-db15ff30d72c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317416} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408164}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Vector Is in the Column Space of a Larger Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "4b1277a3-a28d-5ae0-951f-c3f5b643aec1" :step/math-academy-id 17494} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120749 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119855 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119670 :question/id #uuid "b6d52e39-a8a0-5048-80a1-a342a2617e0f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6208"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a557ee8d-51a9-5387-a28c-67ffefed55b4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247190} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323698} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408165}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of an Unknown Given a Vector That’s in the Column Space of a Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "b98e9e59-d7cc-545a-8c21-fb820acf8430" :step/math-academy-id 23239 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120682}}] :activity/title "The Column Space of a Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120044}]}
